Snowflake Interview Question (Intern) – Product Revenue & Customer Count (HashMap + Set
I recently got this question in a Snowflake interview and wanted to share it.

Problem Statement

You are given two datasets:

 - Products (as a vector of pairs):
Each entry contains:
product_id
price

 - Sales (as a list of records):
Each entry contains:
customer_id
product_id
quantity
Task

For each product, compute:

Number of unique customers who bought the product and also if one customer bought multiple product or if one prodcut was bought 1 guy and same product was bought by another guy then it should say 2 guy bought this product with the total revenue generated by the prodcut

Return/output in the format:

<number_of_customers> <product_id> <total_revenue>

- Approach

Convert the products vector into a hashmap:

product_id → price
Iterate over sales:
Lookup price using product_id
Compute revenue = price × quantity
Aggregate revenue per product
Track unique customers using a set
Output results per product



// Given as vector of pairs
    vector<pair<int, int>> products = {
        {101, 10},
        {102, 20},
        {103, 15}
    };

// sales: {customer_id, product_id, quantity}
    vector<tuple<int, int, int>> sales = {
        {1, 101, 5},
        {2, 103, 2},
        {1, 103, 1}
    }

    // Convert to map
    unordered_map<int, int> priceMap;
    for (auto &p : products) {
        priceMap[p.first] = p.second;
    }



    unordered_map<int, long long> revenueMap;
    unordered_map<int, unordered_set<int>> customersMap;

    // Process sales
    for (auto &[customer_id, product_id, quantity] : sales) {
        int price = priceMap[product_id];
        long long revenue = price * quantity;

        revenueMap[product_id] += revenue;
        customersMap[product_id].insert(customer_id);
    }

    // Output
    for (auto &[product_id, revenue] : revenueMap) {
        cout << customersMap[product_id].size() << " "
             << product_id << " "
             << revenue << endl;
    }

    return 0;
}
